# Dangling `@evidence[[...]]` links to fix\n- `figure_manifest.json.prov.json` <artifact.sha256> -> Recorded sha256 does not match file contents.; expected=ad5df6dd1b00f493e18b44ab556b5d08da9d30b9d3cfa363beb215d7293244d8; actual=27505d5212d7e5c1e6c9bf68c9397e6105c7b3aa299f60bf714d0972f95ef718\n\n### Evidence hard errors\n- scientific-evidence verifier found 1 dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link(s). figure_manifest.json.prov.json <artifact.sha256> -> Recorded sha256 does not match file contents.; expected=ad5df6dd1b00f493e18b44ab556b5d08da9d30b9d3cfa363beb215d7293244d8; actual=27505d5212d7e5c1e6c9bf68c9397e6105c7b3aa299f60bf714d0972f95ef718 Fix or remove every dangling link, rerun `scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <paper_dir>`, and repeat until zero dangling references remain.\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `pass`\n- finding_count: `0`\n- summary: Main and supplementary claims match the retained results, readable implementation evidence, and final qualification records; verifier warnings do not contradict visible claims.\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\n    \"path\": \"figure_manifest.json\",\n    \"kind\": \"derived\",\n    \"sha256\": \"ad5df6dd1b00f493e18b44ab556b5d08da9d30b9d3cfa363beb215d7293244d8\"\n  },\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[build_artifacts.py]]\"\n  ],\n  \"generated_by\": {\n    \"script_path\": \"build_artifacts.py\",\n    \"command\": [\n      \"python\",\n      \"build_artifacts.py\"\n    ],\n    \"cwd\": \".\"\n  }\n}\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"study_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/study_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/study_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"width_decay_outcomes\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/width_decay_outcomes.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/width_decay_outcomes.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2,\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"censoring_audit\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 13.5,\n        \"legend\": 13.5,\n        \"ticks\": 13.5,\n        \"title\": 14\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/censoring_audit.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/censoring_audit.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n# Paper Strategy\n\n**Status:** ready for `paper_standalone` using `write-paper-standalone`; strategy only, not a manuscript.\n\n**Provisional title:** Memorization Without Observed Grokking Across MLP Widths\n\n## Central Question and Thesis\n\n**Question.** Within a fixed training horizon, how do hidden width and weight decay affect memorization and observed generalization in modular-arithmetic MLPs, and what can a censored width-lag contrast actually establish?\n\n**Thesis.** Across the tested addition and subtraction conditions, wider fixed-rate MLPs attain sustained memorization earlier or attain it when the narrowest models do not. None attains sustained generalization within 20,000 updates. The reported width-lag contrast consequently reflects fitting and endpoint substitution, not an identified effect on grokking time.\n\nThis is a finite-horizon negative-results paper with a metric-identification analysis, not a new scheduling algorithm or a demonstrated neural mechanism. Its strength is a clear separation between what the experiments observe and what their lag statistic cannot establish. It partially answers the original research question; it does not complete the original proposed mechanistic investigation. Readiness means a coherent grounded paper is possible, not that ICLR acceptance or a novelty claim has been established.\n\n## Evidence and Framing Decision\n\nNo prior manuscript, resolved edit-context input, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` directory is present. The JSON status `recentered` describes a fresh shift from the source proposal to the observed scientific setting, not an edit to an existing paper. No inherited title or figure plan exists to preserve.\n\nThree framings were compared:\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision | Grounded reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Preserve the proposed capacity-versus-decay-clock explanation | Reject as the thesis | Random-label fitting controls, initial-logit calibration, and the proposed generalization-time equivalence test were not performed. |\n| Broaden into an annealing or optimizer-method family | Reject as the thesis | A selected fixed recipe has final evidence, but alternate schedules and the original lower-rate implementation lack matched final-plane comparisons. Search scores cannot establish final superiority or a width mechanism. |\n| Recenter on the final width-by-decay factorial and censoring boundary | Select | The final evaluator supplies all condition-level training traces and the event/aggregation definitions needed to explain the negative result and audit the lag contrast. |\n\nThe final evidence is independent of the source proposals and recent memory. `downstream_study` is absent; no `study.*` grounding sources are supplied. Do not invent a campaign selection contract, search score differences, or scored/unscored attempt counts. Omit a comparison against the original learning-rate-0.001 implementation because no comparable final-plane result is retained. Missing performance evidence is not a loss.\n\n### Private Qualification and Provenance Audit\n\nThe authoritative `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct` values are **completed**, **qualified**, and **true**, respectively. Do not replace these with an earlier search acceptance or failure label. Record the completed, accepted evaluation in a reader-facing reproducibility disclosure without calling it evidence of generalization, scientific validity, superiority, or a causal mechanism. If revised authoritative artifacts instead record failed qualification, disclose that failure throughout writing, review, audit, and revision while preserving all limitations; the cause remains unknown without diagnostics.\n\nThe local final evaluator and evidence JSON hashes match the final metadata. The available constant-rate implementation specifies learning rate 0.01; its connection to the final evaluation is supported by retained source and campaign records, but an independently reconstructed match to the final program-tree hash has not been established. Keep this distinction explicit in the private audit. The source labels candidate diagnostics as unverified. The scientific observations used here are retained evaluator-generated traces, not candidate-reported measurements; do not extrapolate this into comprehensive integrity or adaptivity certification.\n\n### Actual Evaluated Setting\n\n- Modulus 97; addition and subtraction, not the proposal's alternate modulus or data fraction.\n- Concatenated one-hot inputs and a linear–ReLU–linear network with hidden widths 16, 64, and 256; measured parameter counts 4769, 18785, and 74849.\n- Full-batch cross-entropy training with AdamW, decay 0 or 1, the fixed constant-rate policy, and a 20,000-update horizon.\n- Three initialization seeds: 1009, 2027, 3037. Each seed episode contains twelve condition records; do not call the three episodes three training runs or treat all 36 condition records as independent replications.\n- Each operation's deterministic final pool contains 4705 pairs, split into 1411 training and 3294 held-out pairs. Splits are fixed across seeds. This is not 30% training on all 97-squared input pairs.\n- Checkpoints every 500 updates. Memorization requires training accuracy at least 0.99 at three consecutive checkpoints; generalization uses held-out accuracy at least 0.95 over the same persistence rule. The event is dated to the first checkpoint of the qualifying triplet.\n\nMethod provenance: hash-matched `task/evaluate_final.py`, `task/resources/final/benchmark.json`, `task/requirements.txt`, and the retained constant-rate source. Scientific observations and deterministic summaries are grounded in `final.evidence.episodes`; named metrics use `final.metric.*`. Do not manufacture additional source identifiers for unregistered code files.\n\n## Scientific Explanation and Its Testability\n\nUse a deliberately limited accounting explanation: finite-horizon width differences in fitting can generate apparent lag differences even when no generalization time is observed. The stored event table tests the fitting part; the evaluator's exact formula tests the accounting part. This is a retrospective interpretation, not a preregistered mechanism test.\n\nThe evidence gives 30 memorization events among 36 condition records and no sustained generalization event. Widths 64 and 256 memorize at checkpoint 500 in every condition. Width 16 without decay memorizes at checkpoint 1000 or 1500; width 16 with positive decay never meets the threshold. Checkpoint ties do not imply equal continuous-time dynamics.\n\nLet T be the training horizon, M the sustained memorization checkpoint, and G the sustained generalization checkpoint. The reported lag is T if M is absent; otherwise it is max(0, (G if observed else T) − M). When G is absent but M exists, this is the observed remaining follow-up after fitting, not an observed delay to generalization. When M is absent, a post-memorization delay is not even defined: T is a scoring convention.\n\nUnder positive decay, width 16 receives T, whereas width 256 receives T−500. Their difference is therefore exactly 500 for every seed. The supplied interval [500, 500] reflects identical values of this capped statistic, not precise knowledge of latent grokking times. The reported decay interaction, 1083.3333333333335 updates, has the same limitation. Explain these facts in the body, not only the supplement.\n\nAn observed generalization event or failure to reproduce this formula would defeat the present account of these stored contrasts. A causal explanation of why the networks do not generalize would require new interventions, which are out of scope here. The original claim about near-invariant true generalization times across width remains untested.\n\n## Included Method Groups\n\n1. **Fixed-rate width family without weight decay — baseline.** The within-evaluation reference, not the original lower-rate source program.\n2. **Matched width family with unit decay — ablation.** The evaluated regularization contrast at the same widths, operations, seeds, and horizon; no claim of scale-matched initialization.\n3. **Nonlearning prediction references — supporting.** Initial network, training-label mode, exact-pair lookup with mode fallback, and label-deranged-input predictions. Disjoint held-out pairs make the lookup and mode references identical. Derangement uses held-out labels to construct a diagnostic permutation and is not a deployable learned comparator or a random-label training experiment.\n\nDo not include proposal-only width grids, independent random-label networks, calibration procedures, mechanistic circuits, or a sequence of discarded scheduling branches as required methods.\n\n## Results Organization\n\nBoth operations are condition-specific evidence for one thesis. Organize by scientific comparison, not by experiment order:\n\n1. **Width and Decay Effects on Fitting.** A twelve-row main event table shows each operation/decay/width condition, all three memorization times or non-attainment, and observed generalization count. Preserve the six narrow positive-decay fitting failures.\n2. **Held-Out Outcomes.** Compare final-five-checkpoint training and held-out accuracy across width. Show the individual seed observations; contextualize held-out results using the available reference predictors. The stored per-record score is the final-five-checkpoint held-out mean, not an event-attainment rate.\n3. **What the Capped Lag Measures.** Give the exact formula, the supplied contrast and degenerate interval, and the decay interaction with their identification caveats. These are comparisons over width, not learning-curve progression.\n\nIf reporting the aggregate control gap, define it as the equally weighted late-window accuracy difference against four stored references, two of which coincide. Do not imply four independent controls. Avoid pooled significance tests, unsupported generalization-rate confidence intervals, or imputation of unobserved events as actual event times. Only three initialization seeds vary; neither dataset split nor operation supplies independent seed replication.\n\n## Required Figures and Readability\n\nThe inspected ICLR template uses `paper.sty`: **one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height**. A full-width figure is one text-column wide; do not assume a two-column conference format. All three required figures use the full text width. A nominal half-width figure would be only 2.75 inches wide and is inappropriate for these panel/annotation densities. Heights below are design budgets, not measured results.\n\n| Figure | Scientific content and generator | Budget and placement |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `study_overview` | One early design-and-inference overview using `generate-concept-figure`: the evaluated factorial, the two distinct event definitions, and the finite-horizon inference boundary. No fabricated curves or causal mechanisms. | Full width, page 1 or 2; 2 compartments, at most 2 across; at most 6 categories and 3 series per compartment; minimum 9-point rendered labels; graphic height about 1.7–2.0 inches plus caption. |\n| `width_decay_outcomes` | Matplotlib seed dotplots. Columns: addition/subtraction. Rows: late-window training/held-out accuracy. Width on the horizontal axis, decay in two fixed colors, three seed markers and condition mean. | Full width; 4 panels in 2-by-2 layout; 3 width categories and 2 series per panel; minimum 8.5-point labels; graphic height about 3.1–3.5 inches plus caption. Match scales within rows; use separate row scales so held-out values remain visible. |\n| `censoring_audit` | Matplotlib direct comparison of capped lag by width. One panel per decay condition; operation-specific neutral shapes/patterns and individual seeds. Explicitly mark missing memorization and state that generalization is unobserved throughout. | Full width; 2 panels in one row; 3 categories and 2 series per panel; minimum 9-point labels; graphic height about 2.0–2.3 inches plus caption. A labeled focused vertical range is acceptable; an apparent event at the cap is not. |\n\nUse one overview workflow only; orchestration, not this strategy stage, owns its execution. Keep Figure 2's decay colors semantically stable; Figure 3 can distinguish operations with neutral shapes rather than reassign those colors. No trajectory figure is required: direct outcomes and event tables support the thesis without a run diary or compressed learning curves.\n\n**Export contract.** Quantitative plots must have editable/reproducible plotting source, vector PDF, and 300-DPI PNG. Reference PDF from LaTeX whenever present. Require at least 2400 pixels for full-width raster output or 1200 for partial/column-width raster-only output. For a 5.5-inch final graphic, render the source canvas large enough to meet the PNG pixel budget with proportionally scaled typography, rather than upscaling a saved image. Preserve editable draw.io and any PDF export for the overview; use raster only if no PDF exists and meet the full-width pixel budget.\n\n## Body Layout and Scientific Value\n\nTarget about **8.5 body pages**, excluding references and supplement, without margin or font changes. An eight-page body must finish at least 75% full under `VENUE.md`.\n\n| Component | Approximate body-page allocation, figures included |\n| --- | --- |\n| Abstract, Introduction, and early overview | 1.5 |\n| Related Work | 0.75 |\n| Experimental Design and Event Definitions | 1.75 |\n| Results, both quantitative figures, and event table | 3.0 |\n| Discussion and Limitations | 1.25 |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 |\n\nUse the space for a precise fitting-versus-generalization question, exact event definitions, paired-seed evidence, readable comparisons, reference-predictor interpretation, the capped-lag derivation, and limits of identification. Do not fill pages with implementation history, repeated zero-event panels, literature padding, or unexecuted experiments. If the grounded material cannot fill the venue budget substantively, surface that conflict rather than disguise it.\n\nThe template scaffold contains a named author and final-copy command; these are incompatible with anonymous review. The later writer must use anonymous review mode, omit author identities/affiliations/acknowledgements/funding and identifying links, preserve the required orchestrator watermark, and check ICLR 2026 submission compliance. The old style-file header alone does not establish latest-template provenance. Do not invent a venue checklist.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nKeep the decisive negative evidence and capped-lag limitation in the main paper. After the references, use the prescribed clear-page/appendix/Supplementary Information structure for:\n\n1. **Exact Data and Training Specification.** Deterministic data construction and final-pool counts; actual one-hot representation and network parameterization; optimizer settings; seed and checkpoint definitions. Source registry anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`, supplemented by the inspected final evaluator, resource JSON, and dependency file.\n2. **Complete Condition-Level Observations.** All 36 records with events or non-attainment, capped lag, late-window accuracies, and completion. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.completion_fraction`, `final.metric.delayed_fraction`.\n3. **Reference Predictors and Available Diagnostics.** Separate stored reference accuracies and the duplicate-control explanation; descriptive endpoint loss/norm tables only. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.control_gap`. No new mechanism test is implied by these diagnostics.\n4. **Capped-Lag Calculation and Reproduction Map.** Exact event/aggregation pseudocode, three-seed paired contrast and Student-t interval construction, and artifact-to-script/table/figure mapping. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.restricted_mean_lag`, `final.metric.width_lag_contrast`, `final.metric.width_lag_ci_low`, `final.metric.width_lag_ci_high`, `final.metric.decay_interaction`. Document `numpy==2.1.3` and `torch==2.5.1+cpu`, and distinguish training from replotting stored results. The evaluator requires an RPC execution context; do not invent a working standalone command or undocumented hardware/runtime claim.\n5. **LLM Usage.** A separate section discloses artifact-supported roles in idea proposal, bounded policy implementation, reference-query planning, framing, and writing, while distinguishing evaluator-owned training/measurements. Do not claim undocumented human review or model identities. Anchor to the final evidence and named local implementation/query-plan provenance; do not present an episode as sole proof of writing roles.\n\nAny subsequently supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses belong here by default. Do not guess their ranking, promote or discard them to satisfy page counts, or retroactively alter this strategy on that basis.\n\n## Limits, Reference Safety, and Writing Guardrails\n\nThe empirical scope is one modulus, two related operations, one representation/architecture, three widths and three initialization seeds, a fixed split per operation, two decay values, one selected learning-rate recipe, and a finite horizon. Initialization scale is not matched across widths. There are no independently trained random-label controls, circuit measurements, or true generalization-time observations. Late threshold excursions without enough following checkpoints cannot meet the sustained-event rule. Non-attainment does not imply that grokking never occurs, and low accuracy alone does not identify the cause.\n\nReference files contain retrieved metadata, including off-target entries; metadata retrieval is not proof of consultation. Only exact starter-literature identities verified against both bibliography and provenance may be used, and substantive claims require actually consulting the approved sources. Never identify a paper by a query-string match, expand citation graphs, or conduct broad width-grokking searches. This strategy made no external lookup and inspected local reference metadata only. Stop and log any identifiable prohibited encounter; do not infer the forbidden paper's identity. No novelty claim may depend on pretending that an unrestricted literature review was performed.\n\n**Forbidden headings:** Campaign Progression; Search Timeline; Milestones; First Successful Run; Phase 1 / Phase 2; Experiment A / Experiment B; Winning Configuration; Successive Improvements; Capacity-versus-Decay-Clock Mechanism Confirmed.\n\n**Forbidden public phrasing:** “we then tried,” “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “next-best,” “later variants,” “search winner proves,” pipeline-native metric/acceptance labels listed in the JSON contract, “larger models grok faster,” “grokking never occurs,” “the confidence interval proves faster generalization,” claims of random-label control experiments, and “state of the art.” Internal field names and paths appear above only as private audit or source-routing instructions, not recommended manuscript language.\n\nPrefer explicit reader-facing names: memorization attainment, generalization attainment, late-window held-out accuracy, and administratively capped lag. Keep the two sustained thresholds distinct. A completed accepted evaluation is compatible with a scientifically negative observation; publication eligibility cannot upgrade that observation into evidence for a neural mechanism.\n\n## Stage Boundary\n\nWrite only the strategy artifacts and append-only memory records in this stage. Do not draft `paper.tex`, create `claims_grounding.json`, render figures, launch new experiments, or run final-paper validation. Validate the strategy's JSON structure, registered source IDs, figure budgets, and consistency with existing evidence only.\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Memorization Without Observed Grokking Across MLP Widths}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\TM}{T_{\\mathrm{M}}}\n\\newcommand{\\TG}{T_{\\mathrm{G}}}\n\\newcommand{\\LH}{L_H}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nDoes increasing model size change delayed generalization, or only the speed of fitting the training data? We study this distinction in full-batch ReLU multilayer perceptrons on modular addition and subtraction. A fixed learning-rate protocol crosses three hidden widths with two AdamW weight-decay settings and three initialization seeds, producing 36 completed training records. Memorization and generalization are defined by separate sustained accuracy thresholds. Within 20,000 updates, 30 records attain memorization but none attains generalization. Wider networks fit earlier without decay and fit where the narrowest networks fail with positive decay; these differences do not establish faster grokking. An audit of the reported endpoint-substituted lag shows that its positive 500-update width contrast, including a degenerate seed interval, follows from fitting times and censoring rather than observed generalization. The evidence supports a finite-horizon separation between width-dependent fitting and unobserved generalization, while leaving the eventual dependence of grokking time on model size unidentified.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_contrast]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nSmall algorithmic datasets offer an unusually transparent setting for investigating generalization: the target rule is known, every possible input can be enumerated, and training performance can be separated from performance on unseen inputs. Yet fitting such data need not reveal when a network learns the underlying operation. Grokking names the striking case in which generalization emerges long after the training data have been fitted \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. This separation raises a basic question about scale: does a larger network change the arrival of generalization, or simply change the point from which the delay is measured?\n\nThe distinction matters because a delay is a difference between event times. Earlier memorization can increase a measured delay even if generalization is unchanged. Conversely, replacing an unobserved generalization event with the training endpoint can produce an apparently precise contrast without any generalization event having occurred. A study of model size therefore needs to report fitting, generalization, and non-attainment separately, rather than infer one from another. Training accuracy alone is particularly inadequate as a proxy for learning an algorithm; the broader distinction between finite-sample fitting and useful generalization is well established \\citep{Zhang2017Rethinking}.\n\nWe examine a controlled width-by-decay grid for a single fixed optimization recipe on addition and subtraction modulo 97. The models are one-hidden-layer ReLU networks with concatenated one-hot inputs. Hidden width varies over 16, 64, and 256; all other architectural choices are fixed. Two decay settings and three initialization seeds give 36 training records. Our working explanation is deliberately limited: width may remove a fitting bottleneck without making generalization observable within the same computational horizon. A stronger claim, that width changes an eventual generalization clock, requires events that this study does not observe.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L789-L871]]\n\nThe results make this separation concrete. All wider networks attain sustained memorization at the first noninitial checkpoint. Narrow networks fit later without decay and do not attain memorization with positive decay. Nevertheless, no condition reaches sustained held-out accuracy of 95\\% within 20,000 updates. Thus, the observed size dependence is a fitting result, not an estimate of the size dependence of grokking time.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nOur contributions are an explicit finite-horizon width comparison across related modular operations, a complete seed-level account of fitting and generalization non-attainment, and an algebraic audit of an endpoint-substituted lag statistic. We show why a nonzero width contrast and a zero-width seed interval can coexist with no observed generalization. These findings delimit what the evaluated configuration establishes; they neither rule out later grokking nor choose among proposed neural mechanisms.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_low]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/width_lag_ci_high]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n  \\caption{Study design and observation boundary. Addition and subtraction modulo 97 are evaluated under a shared fixed-rate MLP protocol crossing three hidden widths, two weight-decay settings, and three initialization seeds. Memorization requires training accuracy of at least 99\\%; generalization requires held-out accuracy of at least 95\\%, each for three consecutive checkpoints. Non-attainment at 20,000 updates is not a measured generalization delay. The diagram summarizes the design, not a proposed neural circuit.}\n  \\label{fig:overview}\n  % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n  % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L721-L743]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Grokking and candidate explanations.}\n\\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} establish delayed generalization on small algorithmically generated datasets and describe its dependence on dataset size. Our experiment uses this setting to distinguish finite-budget fitting from generalization-event timing; it is not a replication of a particular delayed-generalization trajectory. \\citet{Liu2023Omnigrok} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm and demonstrate its presence beyond algorithmic data. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} propose competition between faster memorizing solutions and more norm-efficient generalizing solutions. \\citet{Kumar2024LazyRich} analyze a transition from lazy to feature-learning dynamics, including a two-layer polynomial-regression setting. These explanations motivate keeping optimization, regularization, and representation effects distinct. Our recorded norms and losses do not measure circuit efficiency or a lazy-to-rich transition, and cannot discriminate these accounts.\n\n\\paragraph{Capacity and generalization need not move together.}\n\\citet{Zhang2017Rethinking} demonstrate that fitting training labels and explaining generalization are different problems. \\citet{Nakkiran2020DoubleDescent} show that increasing model size or training duration need not yield monotone test performance. \\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a common pattern-learning-speed perspective on grokking and double descent, including variation in capacity. This context cautions against treating a width effect on training accuracy as sufficient evidence about held-out learning. Our grid is too sparse, and its accuracies too uniformly low, to establish a double-descent curve or a general capacity law.\n\n\\paragraph{Regularization and the role of the learning rate.}\nAdamW decouples weight decay from the loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. However, its per-update parameter shrinkage still depends on the learning rate used in that update. We therefore compare decay arms under one shared constant rate rather than interpret a change in the learning-rate recipe as a clean intervention on decay alone. The literature discussion is restricted to exact, permitted starter sources; we make no exhaustive priority or novelty claim from this restricted coverage.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem, Data, and Evaluation Target}\n\nFor a pair $(a,b)\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}^2$, the target is either $(a+b)\\bmod p$ or $(a-b)\\bmod p$, with $p=97$. Each operation has $p^2=9,409$ possible ordered pairs. The final evaluation uses a deterministic subset of 4,705 pairs for each operation, with 1,411 training pairs and 3,294 held-out pairs. Thus the training set is approximately 30\\% of the retained subset, not 30\\% of the entire operation table; it covers approximately 15\\% of all possible inputs. Addition and subtraction use independently constructed operation-specific partitions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L613]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/benchmark.json]]\n\nThe partition is reproducible without downloading data. Each input and operation are encoded in canonical JSON, normalized, and hashed. Sorting the hashes separates the development subset from the final subset. A second hash ordering with a fixed training prefix assigns the training portion within the final subset. The split is fixed across initialization seeds and widths. Consequently, seed variability measures training-initialization variability on a fixed split, not uncertainty over fresh data partitions. The held-out set is used for recording performance, not for gradient updates. Appendix~\\ref{app:construction} specifies the construction and readable source locations.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L588-L631]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L825-L870]]\n\nWe report both operations rather than treating subtraction as an unseen transfer task. Each network is independently trained from scratch on its own operation. The shared architecture and optimization recipe permit a condition-specific replication of the width comparison, but not a claim of cross-task representation transfer.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L656-L660]]\n\n\\subsection{Working Explanation and Falsifiable Predictions}\n\nThe working explanation separates \\emph{fitting capacity under the chosen optimizer} from \\emph{learning a held-out algorithm}. Increasing width can make the finite training table easier to fit without supplying evidence that the network acquires the arithmetic rule. In a regularized setting, the fitting requirement may become more demanding, so that the narrowest network no longer attains the same training criterion even though wider networks do. This is an explanation of operational fitting behavior, not a claim that parameter count alone determines representational capacity.\n\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts:\ncompile_latex.sh\nvalidate_compile_inputs.py\nvalidate_figures.py\nvalidate_rendered_si.py\nvalidate_tex_refs.py\n\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts:\nvalidate_claims.py\n/workspace/investigation\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
